# Kidney representation and uncertainty in AlphaGenome regulatory variant prediction

**Christopher Lawrence** BSc, MBBS, LLM, MD(Res), FRCP
Consultant Nephrologist, 9 Harley Street, London W1G 9QY, United Kingdom
ORCID 0000-0002-8159-0879
Correspondence: Christopher.lawrence3@nhs.net

---

## Abstract

Sequence-to-function models predict regulatory activity from DNA and are proposed
for interpreting non-coding variants, but their behaviour in kidney has received
little attention. We asked what AlphaGenome exposes for kidney, how it performs
on kidney expression quantitative trait loci (eQTL), and whether performance
depends on choosing the kidney output. We audited the model's human output tracks
against three developer sources and selected 32,818 fine-mapped variant-gene
pairs across all 49 GTEx tissues in the EMBL-EBI eQTL Catalogue, of which 31,934
were scored. Kidney parenchyma accounts for 103 of 5,563 output tracks (1.85%).
All four kidney transcription-factor tracks assay CTCF, and cell-type-resolved
accessibility is limited to seven DNase tracks, only one from a documented adult
donor. Kidney cortex yielded the fewest eligible pairs of any tissue, 59 against
a median of 616, at all 36 thresholds tested. Predicted and observed effects
correlated at rho 0.505 (95% CI 0.269 to 0.684), unremarkable once other tissues
are downsampled to the same size. Re-scoring the same variants with every tissue
output in turn, kidney ranked sixth of 54; no comparator showed a significantly
higher correlation in paired comparisons, and kidney was nominally higher than 10
of 53 on intervals unadjusted for multiplicity. Kidney is sparsely represented
and is the least well evidenced tissue in this benchmark. Whether that reflects
shared regulatory effects or limited use of tissue identity cannot be determined
from these data.

**Keywords:** chronic kidney disease; gene expression regulation; genomics;
machine learning; quantitative trait loci

---

## 1. Introduction

Most of the heritable variation in kidney function is mediated by variants in
regulatory elements rather than in protein-coding sequence [1], and their
interpretation depends on knowing which element is disrupted and in which cell
type. Coding and regulatory signals converge on the same genes: across 1026 loci
in 2.2 million individuals, 1363 coding variants disrupted 782 genes, of which
601 were also targeted by regulatory variants [2]. Fine-mapping rarely resolves a
single causal variant, and in a meta-analysis of 1,201,909 individuals only 44 of
634 independent signals did so [3].

Sequence-to-function models trained on large compendia of functional genomic data
predict regulatory activity from DNA alone [4-6], and are increasingly proposed
as a route to interpreting such variants [7]. Earlier models of this family
perform less well in regions that distinguish one cell type from another [8],
but that work evaluated Enformer and Sei and predates the model examined here.

The question matters for nephrology because kidney traits are moderately to
highly heritable [9] and that heritability is compartment-specific: 56% of
common-variant kidney function heritability localises to candidate regulatory
elements of the tubule epithelium and a further 7% to podocyte elements [1].

We asked three questions. What does AlphaGenome expose for kidney? How does it
perform on kidney eQTLs relative to other tissues? And does prediction
performance depend on choosing the kidney output?

## 2. Materials and Methods

### 2.1 Model, versions and dates

AlphaGenome [6] was accessed through its programmatic interface (alphagenome
Python package v0.9.0) between 13 and 14 September 2026, under the developers'
non-commercial research terms. Coordinates are GRCh38 and gene identifiers follow
the annotation returned by the interface. All scoring used the recommended
RNA-seq variant scorer (GeneMaskLFCScorer) with the model's maximum context of
1,048,576 bases centred on the variant; shorter contexts were not used. Stranded
gene tracks were merged, which is the interface default.

### 2.2 Output track inventory

The human output manifest was obtained from three developer sources: the
dataframe embedded in the published notebook, the protobuf manifest shipped with
the research code, and the deployed interface. These are three representations of
the same developer metadata; their agreement establishes internal consistency,
not independent biological validation.

The protobuf manifest contains 6126 entries, of which 563 are placeholders named
"Padding" that pad each output head to a round size. After their removal the
manifest reconciles exactly, modality by modality, with the notebook dataframe
and the live interface, at 5563 tracks.

**Reconciliation with the primary publication.** AlphaGenome is reported as
predicting 5930 human tracks [6]. The difference of 367 is exactly the
splice-junction count. Splice junctions are the only splice modality the
interface exposes unstranded, whereas splice site usage is exposed as 734 tracks,
being 367 per strand. Counting junctions per strand gives 5563 + 367 = 5930,
reconciling the totals exactly. Kidney's share is 103/5563 (1.85%) as exposed and
117/5930 (1.97%) under the publication's convention; we report both.

Track counts are not measures of training influence. Tracks differ in redundancy,
assay type and contribution to training, and biosample labels are not necessarily
independent donors or experiments. We report output track coverage, which is what
these sources permit, and make no inference about training weight.

### 2.3 Biosample classification

Biosamples were assigned by explicit curated name, never by substring matching.
Matching on "renal" captures adrenal gland (27 tracks) and perirenal adipose (4
tracks); testing for renal papilla with the unqualified term "papilla" returns
false positives from fungiform papilla and hair follicle dermal papilla.

*Kidney parenchyma* comprises the nephron-bearing organ and its resident cell
types. *Urinary tract* (ureter, renal pelvis, urothelium) is reported separately.
*Kidney cell line* covers HEK293 and HEK293T, reported separately because HEK293,
although derived from embryonic kidney [10], expresses neurofilament subunits and
other neuronal proteins and is of debated lineage [11]. A guard function fails the
analysis if an uncurated kidney-like name appears in a future release.

### 2.4 ENCODE inventory

Human experiments were retrieved from the ENCODE portal [12] on 13 September 2026
using the organ facet biosample_ontology.organ_slims=kidney, restricted to Homo
sapiens and released status, and classified into tissue or primary cell versus
cell line by the same curated approach. Conclusions are restricted to the
experiments this query returns. A facet query cannot establish that no further
kidney data exist in ENCODE, still less in other repositories or publications.

### 2.5 Benchmark variant set

Ground truth came from the EMBL-EBI eQTL Catalogue [13], which re-quantifies all
49 GTEx tissues [14] through one pipeline under a CC BY 4.0 licence. The
catalogue's GTEx is version 8. We used version 8 for comparability with the
benchmark reported in the primary publication, not because later releases were
unavailable.

Positives were variants in a SuSiE [15] credible set with posterior inclusion
probability at least 0.9 and credible-set size at most 5, taken from the
catalogue's susie files using the reported per-variant pip and cs_size fields
without aggregation across components. The probability threshold selects variants
with strong posterior support for inclusion. The size cap is a separate and more
conservative restriction requiring that fine-mapping resolved the signal; it is
not implied by the probability threshold. Sensitivity to both is reported for
the number of eligible pairs, not for the correlations.
The unit of observation is the variant-gene pair within a tissue. This yielded
32,818 pairs across 49 tissues. The benchmark set was constructed and frozen
before any variant was scored.

### 2.6 Scoring and accounting

For each pair the prediction was taken for the eQTL's own target gene in the
output track corresponding to that variant's own GTEx tissue. Tissue
correspondence was curated by hand for the 16 of 49 catalogue sample groups that
do not match a track label by normalisation alone.

**Allele orientation.** The catalogue's variant identifier encodes reference and
alternate alleles, and these were passed unchanged as the reference and alternate
bases of the scored variant. The catalogue reports the effect size with respect
to the alternate allele, and the scorer returns the log fold change of alternate
against reference, so both quantities are oriented to the same allele by
construction. No sign reversals were applied and no pairs were excluded on
orientation grounds. Direction is compared as the sign of the predicted log fold
change against the sign of the reported coefficient; no rescaling was attempted
and the two are not on a common scale (section 4).

Of 32,818 pairs, 32,759 were submitted in a run covering 48 tissues and 59 in a
separate run for kidney cortex; 31,876 and 58 respectively returned a usable
prediction, giving 31,934 analysed pairs and 884 failures (2.69%). Failures
comprised pairs where the target gene was absent from the returned annotation and
pairs where the request did not complete within three attempts; these were not
recorded separately and are reported together.

### 2.7 Statistics

Agreement is Spearman's rank correlation between predicted log fold change and
observed effect size, with directional agreement as the proportion sharing a
sign. Intervals use the Fisher z transform with the Bonett-Wright standard error
[16] and Wilson intervals for proportions [17]. Because observations may share a
gene or credible set, kidney's interval was additionally estimated by bootstrap
resampling of whole credible sets. To test whether kidney's interval width simply
reflects its size, every other tissue was downsampled to kidney's n over 200
replicates each. To test whether kidney's rank on directional agreement could
arise by chance, tissue proportions were simulated under a common pooled rate at
the observed sample sizes: 5000 simulations drawing a binomial count per tissue at
its own n with success probability equal to the sample-size-weighted pooled rate
(0.693), recording how often kidney attained the highest proportion, with ties
resolved to the first tissue encountered. Bootstraps used 5000 resamples for the
credible-set analysis and 10,000 for the paired track comparison, both with
replacement. The simulation describes the stability of the ranking; it is not a
hypothesis test of kidney's observed directional agreement.

### 2.8 Sensitivity to the choice of tissue output

For each kidney benchmark variant the predicted effect on the same target gene
was extracted from every GTEx tissue track the model exposes, and each track's
predictions were correlated against the observed kidney effect. The model exposes
GTEx-labelled RNA-seq tracks for 54 tissues, five more than the 49 with eQTLs in
the catalogue; the additional tracks are tissues for which GTEx reports expression
but no eQTLs at this release, including kidney medulla, and they are retained as
comparators. RNA-seq tracks carrying no GTEx tissue label were excluded rather
than pooled, since a mean across unrelated biosamples is not a tissue.

Because a difference in correlation between two tracks on the same observations
requires an interval, differences from the kidney track were estimated by
bootstrap resampling of the variant-gene pairs, paired across tracks. To
distinguish similar performance from similar predictions, the tracks' prediction
vectors were also correlated directly with one another.

## 3. Results

### 3.1 Kidney occupies under two per cent of the model's output

Kidney parenchyma accounts for 103 of 5563 output tracks (1.85%), or 117 of 5930
(1.97%) counting splice junctions per strand, across 20 of 715 biosample labels.
Urinary tract structures contribute a further 25 tracks and are excluded. HEK293
and HEK293T together contribute 116 tracks.

Coverage is uneven across assay types (Figure 1): 0.25% of transcription-factor
ChIP tracks, 0.60% of ATAC and 0.72% of histone ChIP, against 3.45% of RNA-seq
and 4.26% of DNase. No kidney contact map or PRO-cap track is exposed.

### 3.2 The only kidney transcription factor represented is CTCF

All four kidney transcription-factor tracks assay CTCF, a factor relevant to
kidney biology but not one that characterises kidney regulatory identity. No
other regulatory factor is directly assayed in kidney. HEK293 alone carries 111
transcription-factor tracks covering 110 distinct factors.

### 3.3 Cell-type-resolved coverage, and a developmental skew

Cell-type-resolved kidney accessibility comprises seven DNase tracks:
glomerular visceral epithelial cell (podocyte, paediatric donor), proximal tubule
epithelial cell, kidney epithelial cell, renal cortical epithelial cell, kidney
tubule cell, kidney capillary endothelial cell and nephron progenitor cell. Only
the kidney tubule cell track is from a documented adult donor; three are of
unrecorded stage and two embryonic. The single kidney ATAC track is whole organ. Each of
these seven cell types is represented by one experiment.

No explicitly annotated track was identified for any of twelve queried kidney
cell types and anatomical structures: collecting duct, thick ascending limb, loop
of Henle, distal convoluted tubule, principal cell, intercalated cell, macula
densa, juxtaglomerular apparatus, parietal epithelial cell, connecting tubule,
inner medulla and renal papilla. These are not twelve independent omissions; they
overlap, and they mix cell types with anatomical structures and nephron segments
(the parietal epithelial cell, for instance, is glomerular rather than distal
nephron). Absence of an explicitly annotated track does not establish that the
corresponding signal is absent from bulk kidney measurements, nor that relevant
features cannot be learned through shared information.

Kidney tracks are developmentally skewed: 37% derive from embryonic tissue against
15% across all tracks, and 25% are adult against 46% overall. Because biosample
labels need not be independent donors, these percentages may reflect repeated
assays from few donors.

### 3.4 Within the ENCODE query, kidney transcription-factor data are sparse

Of 592 human experiments returned under the kidney organ facet, 284 were tissue
or primary cell and 308 cell lines, of which 285 were HEK293 or HEK293T. Of 228
transcription-factor ChIP experiments, 221 were in cell lines covering 218
distinct factors, and 7 were in kidney tissue or primary cells, all assaying
CTCF, deposited between 2011 and 2021.

This describes the experiments the defined query returns. It does not establish
that no further kidney transcription-factor occupancy data exist. ENCODE also
holds one intact Hi-C and two PRO-cap kidney experiments that the model does not
expose, and cell-type-resolved kidney accessibility data exist outside ENCODE
[18, 19] and are likewise not represented. Data availability and model data
selection are therefore both contributors, and this study does not apportion
between them.

### 3.5 Kidney yields the fewest eligible pairs of any tissue

Kidney cortex yielded 59 eligible variant-gene pairs, against a median of 616 across 49
tissues and a maximum of 1756. It was the smallest tissue at every one of 36
combinations of posterior probability (0.5 to 0.99) and credible-set size (1 to
uncapped), consistently 27 to 33-fold below the largest. That sweep establishes
the stability of the count ranking; sensitivity of the correlation and directional
agreement to those thresholds was not assessed.

### 3.6 Performance in kidney, and its uncertainty

Across 31,934 pairs, predicted and observed effects correlated at rho 0.505 in
kidney cortex (95% CI 0.269-0.684), against a median of 0.495 across the
49 tissues (Figure 2). Kidney's
observations were one per gene and one per credible set, and a credible-set
bootstrap gave a similar interval (0.290-0.662). Repeated genes and credible sets
therefore do not inflate precision; dependence between separate credible sets
sharing a locus or correlated variants was not assessed.

Kidney's interval was 0.415 wide against a median of 0.128 elsewhere. Interval
width across tissues tracks the number of pairs scored (Figure 3), and the
difference is the expected consequence of sample size rather than a
kidney-specific property: downsampling every other tissue to n = 58 placed
kidney's correlation at the 56th percentile and its interval width at the 44th
percentile of size-matched replicates. Directional agreement in kidney was 0.776
(95% CI 0.653-0.864), nominally the highest of 49 tissues, but simulation under a
common pooled rate of 0.693 produced kidney as the top tissue in 18.9% of runs.
Neither the apparent precision deficit nor the apparent directional advantage is
interpretable as a kidney-specific effect.

### 3.7 Comparison of kidney eQTL prediction across tissue outputs

Scoring the same kidney pairs with every tissue track in turn, the kidney cortex
track ranked 6th of 54 (rho 0.505; Figure 4). Pituitary (0.566),
thyroid (0.549), endocervix (0.541), tibial nerve (0.539) and fallopian tube
(0.512) gave nominally higher correlations, and the range across all 54 tracks
was 0.320 to 0.566.

Paired bootstrap over the same 58 pairs shows that none of those five differences
excludes zero: the largest, pituitary, is +0.061 (95% CI -0.036 to +0.194). No
comparator therefore showed a significantly higher correlation than kidney.
Kidney's own advantage excluded zero against 10 of the 53 comparators. All 53
intervals are unadjusted for multiplicity, so these are exploratory comparisons.
The kidney output is not uninformative; it is simply not uniquely best on these
variants.

Similar correlations need not mean similar predictions, so the prediction vectors
were compared directly. Their pairwise rank correlation had a median of
0.827 (interquartile range 0.702-0.915, minimum 0.316), and kidney against the
others a median of 0.903 (range 0.602-0.990). Outputs are therefore substantially
but not wholly shared: tissue-level variation exists in the predictions, and did
not give kidney a detectable advantage over the highest-ranked alternatives.

### 3.8 Weighting of the tissue average is immaterial here

In GTEx version 8 kidney cortex has the fewest eQTL samples (73 against a median
of 233) and eGenes (1260 against 9642) of 49 tissues, and is down-weighted
7.71-fold in an eGene-weighted average. The unweighted mean correlation was
0.4915 and the eGene-weighted mean 0.4910. Performance was near-uniform across
tissues (standard deviation 0.0399, range 0.395-0.560), so these two schemes
return nearly the same mean; we do not claim this of every possible weighting.

## 4. Discussion

Kidney is sparsely represented in this model's output, and is the tissue about
which this benchmark can say least. Those are the secure findings.

The more informative result is that no comparator output predicted kidney eQTLs
significantly better than the kidney output, though kidney was nominally better
than 10 of 53 on unadjusted intervals. The tracks' predictions were substantially
though not wholly shared.

Two explanations are compatible with this, and our data do not separate them. The
eQTLs were detected in kidney but need not be kidney-specific, and a model that
predicts shared regulatory effects well would behave exactly this way. Or the
model may under-use tissue identity. Distinguishing these would require ground
truth on variants with demonstrably different effects between tissues. An eQTL
significant in kidney but not elsewhere will not serve, because unequal
statistical power produces that pattern. We therefore report the pattern and
decline to attribute it. For the same reason we do not claim that the
near-uniform performance in section 3.8 reflects limited tissue differentiation:
similar accuracy on different tissue-specific datasets does not establish a common
mechanism. Our observations are consistent with reports on
earlier models of this family, which capture promoter determinants while largely
ignoring distal enhancers [20] and explain individual expression differences
poorly, often predicting the wrong direction of effect [21, 22], and produce tracks more
sequence-determined than the data they model [23]. A preprint reports that
AlphaGenome underestimates the effect sizes of causal fine-mapped eQTLs [24].

Three boundaries on what follows. A correlation of 0.505 among selected
fine-mapped eQTLs is not a statement of adequacy, which would require a defined
task and threshold, and is not evidence of diagnostic or clinical utility.
Kidney's estimate is too imprecise, and no equivalence margin was specified, to
support a claim that kidney matches other tissues. And limited experimental data
is not the only constraint, since cell-type-resolved kidney accessibility data
that do exist [18, 19] are also not represented.

Direction of predicted effect is a mechanistic quantity useful for prioritising
experiments. It is not sufficient for clinical action, which depends additionally
on the target gene, disease mechanism, dosage sensitivity, effect magnitude and
tissue context.

Several limitations bound these results. We evaluated one model on one task;
splicing and chromatin tasks may behave differently. Rank correlation is
insensitive to calibration, so our analysis cannot address reported effect-size
attenuation [24]; relatedly, predicted log fold change and a regression
coefficient on normalised expression are not on a common scale, and we compare
rank and sign only. Bulk kidney cortex eQTLs are a coarse readout of
compartment-specific biology [25], with 12% of glomerular and 26% of
tubulointerstitial eQTLs compartment-specific in one series [26], so a model could
perform adequately in bulk and still be wrong in a specific cell type. Each tissue
contributes a different selection of variants, so cross-tissue comparisons are not
like-for-like beyond the size matching performed here. Our positives require
fine-mapping to have resolved a signal, biasing toward larger effects. We did not
search systematically for prior kidney-specific evaluations, so our statement
that we are not aware of one describes our knowledge, not a documented absence.

Two directions follow. Generating cell-type-resolved kidney regulatory data and
larger kidney eQTL studies [27] would reduce the uncertainty documented here. The
second is evaluation against effects demonstrably specific to a tissue, since a
benchmark of eQTLs merely detected in kidney cannot show whether a tissue-labelled
output uses tissue identity. Whether the model itself requires modification is a
question such an evaluation would have to answer first. Until then, predictions
in kidney should be treated as hypothesis-generating, and tissue-labelled outputs
should not be assumed to carry tissue-specific information without a control of
the kind reported here.

## Data availability

All data analysed in this study are publicly available. Fine-mapped associations
came from the EMBL-EBI eQTL Catalogue release 7 (https://www.ebi.ac.uk/eqtl/,
CC BY 4.0), which re-processes GTEx v8 (dbGaP phs000424). Kidney experiment
records came from the ENCODE portal (https://www.encodeproject.org). The frozen
benchmark set, the curated tissue mapping, the output-track manifests and the
per-tissue prediction tables generated during this study are available in the
kidney-alphagenome repository,
https://github.com/PatientThread/kidney-alphagenome, which will be deposited with
a persistent identifier on publication. Model predictions were obtained under
AlphaGenome's non-commercial research terms and are redistributed subject to
those terms; model weights are not redistributed and were not accessed.

## Code availability

All analysis code is available in the same repository,
https://github.com/PatientThread/kidney-alphagenome, as thirteen numbered scripts
that reproduce every reported number and figure. Seven of them run offline from
the files deposited there; the remainder require an AlphaGenome API key, which is
obtained directly from the developers and is not included.

